#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08151725-c767-5bfa-8cc0-566dac73c7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263073} {:answer/id #uuid "6b75a71e-ff62-5063-9c04-4318296a44fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263928} {:answer/id #uuid "8f591e1b-9777-5d0f-b497-43f656adfa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264230} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6ad8b4-a673-5008-ae07-fbef7600a1fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264645} {:answer/id #uuid "bd888fe4-7f5e-51b1-a53d-363189aedc9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08151725-c767-5bfa-8cc0-566dac73c7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263073} :answer-field/id #uuid "93ddfe34-8e2a-5c0d-ad1d-5d50a7d04569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e9cc9e7-1d62-51d1-8fa2-dd51fa7daa28" :question/math-academy-id "q-130257" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1) & =i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇\\,f(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086fecd5-a126-5f32-9308-1bc895c050b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263080} {:answer/id #uuid "8d43de9a-94e9-5878-939e-2733944f61c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264208} {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} {:answer/id #uuid "b92952ce-92d6-5fe1-84dc-4e5ec973c535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264605} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1aafca-14b7-5d95-9816-988f20570192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1d9c83-156f-56e6-8ce6-3d01bf086064" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae068a0a-3a23-5516-a4b7-13c70903e5db" :question/math-academy-id "q-130263" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i-j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\,i+(2(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215d83fe-4933-5de8-986e-15f0aee87618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517546} {:answer/id #uuid "2d55e643-74fe-5152-bfd8-c76baf2debc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517574} {:answer/id #uuid "6b204e6b-3938-557e-9c36-e6465fac0526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517687} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5a1144-2bb6-52a6-ae6a-f5e1b580463b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517790} {:answer/id #uuid "f5758c55-ad74-5dfd-b215-f4a7c6749383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,3,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215d83fe-4933-5de8-986e-15f0aee87618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517546} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0c283d-e80d-5a92-84dc-65204c4fbaa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f39e417c-6152-5bd7-a05f-5fd83a9d6cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-130272" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,-3{y}^{2},\\,2xz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1,2) & =⟨{2}^{2},-3(1)^{2},2(0)(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨4,-3,0⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446194} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} {:answer/id #uuid "94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446213} {:answer/id #uuid "c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446221} {:answer/id #uuid "c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} :answer-field/id #uuid "716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130811" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446193} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446216} {:answer/id #uuid "b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446218} {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36306" :question/problem "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,-4,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446191} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446210} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8a41f1-5149-587a-ba0e-8dfbff363aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,4,\\,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446212} {:answer/id #uuid "a78d148d-8a70-55a2-a873-68980ae2bd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446215} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6b2ea-bb17-5bc1-89b4-f847433baa23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446210} :answer-field/id #uuid "da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83598" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1304514c-b435-522f-ad3a-591f2559a22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351730} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4fba7a-fe7c-5a9c-9fbe-87782d23df70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{43}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352676} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} {:answer/id #uuid "c94460ac-494e-513e-82ed-c599cb03d029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{43}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353320} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e9e528-e824-57bb-899a-9e75210a8db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f8833b-3f19-5c49-a196-1b1b54521800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8f45df0-069c-5e4a-a4d7-bdd2e15f5467" :question/math-academy-id "q-86429" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3i+5j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}(3i+5j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =12x\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =(12\\cdot 0)\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =2j\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}i+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{34}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e252ff-bff2-5a0c-8722-678c22a7c16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517501} {:answer/id #uuid "070f81d7-bbdd-59f5-971a-c1e85fd656fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517503} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8dff85-9179-52c1-a1eb-e2b80b8d392e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517603} {:answer/id #uuid "636cdbc8-2c6a-553b-a040-1b1eb24c4aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517671} {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6bc3a1-b754-5de6-a15a-17b5690dd1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-86533" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351772} {:answer/id #uuid "215ecffb-b146-5627-91b0-550f671dd2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351867} {:answer/id #uuid "722664ed-33e9-5cc1-b19b-4351c6efb19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352566} {:answer/id #uuid "81a3d5cd-c8fc-5c45-bcca-96eb05f8c947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352695} {:answer/id #uuid "df442e6f-48af-5860-ac3c-76c823ada575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351772} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e73d7d-cb49-50d9-9ae1-9e40c00f9a4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1b68e57-d9ea-56e4-b3b2-4bb6dcbf9139" :question/math-academy-id "q-86537" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{2}+3,\\,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446175} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc668bd-9c56-5b86-a394-e8a7b8069688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2e,\\,{e}^{4}-3,\\,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446192} {:answer/id #uuid "604a5840-27db-5147-b4e6-7928a771885b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{4}-3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446200} {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446202} {:answer/id #uuid "c9528b5a-8771-5176-a89b-c59ec1b7b7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2e-3,\\,-21⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446202} :answer-field/id #uuid "845bc3ce-d9e8-5c27-8b7e-c01d5d7a2ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" :question/math-academy-id "q-86539" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e8ca8f9-1d26-55d7-9b86-8c52d09b0ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517660} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea4930-c661-5fcb-a591-ae748e162fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517804} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a6152f-0b20-5bb6-96d5-09c1f86baab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517870} {:answer/id #uuid "eac115c2-4ed8-51a6-aa73-4f0569e0d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} :answer-field/id #uuid "082380e4-f20f-513e-9d31-b936b5054d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" :question/math-academy-id "q-86540" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ad0a-171c-5322-8cf2-cfca08972fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500510} {:answer/id #uuid "407711da-42ac-56dc-a3f6-af974631b602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500594} {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} {:answer/id #uuid "86e21ca8-366b-5985-a073-18a5cd00683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500749} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c7ab4-1278-5730-804a-3c97ca86d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcae1503-ee5b-5bcc-859b-64edce5a1f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86542" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
